From 00313ea6c23ec78ca94e9b1c2a738b32a0604998 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 03:21:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] feat: adapt the evaluation path to the coupling
 interface, and record T7

The evaluation CLI now accepts the arrival-condition parametrisation and the
execution-feedback observation, and refuses to load a checkpoint against an
observation or action space it never saw -- a 34D policy fed a 31D
observation used to be a silent mismatch. Self-consistency: the
fixed-setpoint control through the wrapper completes 262000 at 113.6 s and
fails 262001 at 38.7 s, matching the 113.5 / 38.6 s already on record.

T7 verdict, recomputed from the four monitor files rather than summarised:
the interface is what made the coupling learnable at all. The previous 4D
parametrisation completed 0 of 1308 episodes over three seeds at 25k
decisions; the new 2D one reaches 17.8% inside its first 10k decisions and
75% over the last hundred. Nothing else changed, so nothing else can explain
it.

Two of four seeds complete nothing, and they are not idle: their return
improves monotonically while their timeout fraction climbs from ~35% to
~68%, their illegal-entry rate matches the learning seeds, and their
reference radius parks at ~7 m instead of closing to ~4.7 m. They learned to
run out the clock rather than to finish -- the hover pathology this
repository already documented, reappearing in the new action space. The
split is settled inside the first fifth of training, which points at
exploration rather than interface capacity.

The feedback ablation does not support the reverse channel: the run without
it is ahead on first success, on every quartile bin and on final return. One
learning seed per arm is far below the three the project rules require, so
this is recorded as unsupported with the point estimate against it, not as a
measured cost.

